# UI appearance section guide

Use this guide for section 5, UI appearance, of a feature's `{{vars.techDesignFile}}`: where the
elements that carry the feature's scenarios appear, what they are, when they are visible or
enabled, their states, and the accessibility and viewport targets. It places behavior; it does not
design screens. Visual design, layout and wording are the implementation's and the design system's
choice, except in strict rows.

## When and which codes

Write it when a scenario of the task is reached through a user interface, when the brief's
`Tech design sections:` lists this section, or when its `Modifiers:` include `ui-design`.
Dev QA sends `DESIGN_GAP` when UI placement changes and this section does not describe it.

| Code | Covers here |
|---|---|
| IC | operability, user error protection, inclusivity (accessibility), self-descriptiveness, user assistance, learnability, recognizability, engagement |
| SAFE | hazard warning: a warning or confirmation before an irreversible or wide-reaching action |
| FLEX | adaptability: viewports, platforms, input methods |

Project-wide targets (accessibility level, supported browsers, smallest viewport) belong to the
baseline; write a feature row only where the feature needs more. UI words (page, dialog, button,
field) are expected here; they are banned only in the requirements.

## What to write

Rows in the [requirement format](../requirement-format.md). Allowed content:

| Allowed | Example |
|---|---|
| Where it appears: page, view, panel, dialog, menu or command, named by purpose; a relative position only when the behavior depends on it | in the cart summary of the cart page; next to each Item |
| What it is, by role: action, link, input, list, message, indicator | the place-order action |
| When it is visible or enabled, as business states from the glossary | disabled while the Cart is empty |
| Its states: empty, loading, error, success, read-only | the order history shows an empty state for a new Customer |
| The information a message carries (not its wording) | the confirmation names the refund amount |
| Accessibility targets | every checkout action operable by keyboard alone |
| Viewports and platforms | usable from 320 CSS px wide without horizontal scrolling |

Every placement row cites the scenario ID it places at the end of the Requirement cell:
`(places CHK-1.2)`. Accessibility and viewport rows apply to the feature's UI as a whole and cite
none. A placement that no scenario needs is unspecified behavior: drop it, or ask for the scenario
(`REQUIREMENTS_GAP`). For a command-line interface the same rules apply: the command that offers
the behavior, when it asks, what it reports on success and failure.

## Never write

- Colours, spacing, element sizes, fonts, icons, animation.
- Component libraries or component names, style classes or tokens, pixel positions, test
  selectors, route paths, file names.
- Copy: labels, messages, help texts. State the information, not the words.
- Screenshots or mock-ups as the requirement; describe the required facts in words.

## Strict UI behaviour (only with `ui-design`)

When the brief's `Modifiers:` include `ui-design` (a bug fix corrects a design decision, or the
user gave explicit design requirements), add a table under `### Strict UI behaviour` after the
other rows, with the columns ID | Sub-characteristic | Before (must not return) | After (exact) |
Verify by | Priority | Source (example below).

- State exactly the properties the user specified or the corrected decision concerns, including
  wording, order or a visual property when that is what the user specified, quoted as given. Add
  nothing the user did not specify; prefer the design system's named role (the destructive action
  style) to raw values when the user agrees.
- The After cell cites the scenario ID it places; the Before cell is the behavior that must not
  come back (so the row stays true after the fix), or "none" for a new element.
- No strict rows without the modifier. When the user dictates exact design during this step,
  ask whether it is a requirement (the implementation decides by default). If it is, add
  `ui-design` to the brief header's `Modifiers:` and log the reason in `## Classification`; the
  route already includes this step.

## Greenfield, adoption, brownfield

- **Greenfield:** there are no screens yet. Place elements by purpose, using names from the
  scenarios and the glossary. Ask once for the accessibility level, supported platforms and the
  smallest viewport, with proposed defaults (WCAG 2.2 AA, the two latest versions of the major
  desktop and mobile browsers, 320 CSS px); they become baseline rows with the user's consent.
- **Adoption and brownfield:** for the touched screens only, read the views where the behavior
  lives, the design system or component library in use (for its vocabulary, never to prescribe
  components), accessibility lint rules or automated checks, the supported browsers and
  breakpoints, and end-to-end tests that pin placement. Write existing placements as rows with
  `Source: existing` only when a scenario of the task depends on them and the user confirms each:
  today's UI may be the bug.
- **Editing an existing section:** change rows in place, keep IDs, move removed or replaced rows to
  section 8, Retired IDs.

## Example

| ID | Sub-characteristic | Requirement | Measure | Verify by | Priority | Source |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| CHK-IC-1 | Operability | The place-order action shall appear in the cart summary of the cart page and be disabled while the Cart is empty (places CHK-1.2) | present; disabled exactly when the Cart is empty | test | Must | user |
| CHK-IC-2 | User error protection | When a Customer cancels a paid Order, a confirmation dialog shall name the refund amount before the Order is cancelled (places CHK-4.1) | no cancellation without confirmation | test | Must | user |
| CHK-IC-3 | Inclusivity | Every checkout action shall be operable by keyboard alone | 0 actions unreachable in a keyboard walkthrough | inspect | Must | user |

Strict row of a ui-design bug fix:

| ID | Sub-characteristic | Before (must not return) | After (exact) | Verify by | Priority | Source |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| CHK-IC-4 | Operability | Removing the last Item sends the Customer to the home page | The cart page stays open and shows the empty-cart state with a link to the catalog (places CHK-2.3) | test | Must | user |

## Staleness pitfalls

- A component name ("drawer", a library's modal): name the role (dialog, panel).
- Layout beyond placement (two columns, sticky header): drop it unless a strict row requires it.
- Wording copied from today's UI: state the information the message carries.
- "The new button" or "now shows": state the placement timelessly.
- Screens the task does not touch: leave them out; this section is not a UI inventory.
